1. Executive Summary
This white paper presents a novel diamond-shaped multi-agent AI framework designed to revolutionize
the detection and prevention of Anti-Money Laundering (AML) activities in financial ecosystems. The
proposed system integrates FinBERT for financial text understanding, SDNL embeddings for
self-supervised representation learning, and a collection of specialized sub-agents built using Graph
Neural Networks (GNNs), Graph Attention Networks (GATs), and rule-based decision models. At its
core, a King Agent governs the data routing and interpretation process, delegating tasks to sub-agents
specializing in transactions, loans, deposits, andKYCverification.
The architecture converges at a Risk Decision Agent, implemented as a Neural Combiner(Deep
Ensemble), which aggregates multi-domain intelligence into a unified AMLrisk score. AGPT-4-based
Virtual Assistant forms the final layer, providing natural language explanations, interpretability, and interactive analytical support to compliance officers. This framework demonstrates how multi-agent
collaboration can provide transparency, adaptability, and efficiency in real-time AML risk management.
2. Introduction
Financial crimes such as money laundering remain one of the most persistent challenges for banking
institutions and regulators. Despite advanced monitoring systems, traditional rule-based AML
frameworks often suffer from high false-positive rates, limited adaptability, and a lack of explainability.
With the explosion of heterogeneous financial data — encompassing transactions, loans, deposits, and
customer identity verification (KYC) — the need for an intelligent, interconnected analytical framework
has become paramount.
Recent advancements in multi-agent systems and graph-based deep learning provide the opportunity
to build intelligent architectures capable of understanding both individual entities and their
relationships. The diamond-shaped multi-agent framework proposed in this paper seeks to achieve this
goal by combining domain-specific intelligence, self-supervised representation learning, and
conversational AI to build a holistic and interpretable AMLsolution.
3. Problem Statement
Existing AML systems rely heavily on static rule sets and human-defined thresholds, which fail to adapt
to evolving laundering patterns. The disjointed nature of KYC,transaction, and loan monitoring leads to
data silos, preventing effective cross-domain risk assessment. Moreover, analysts face a growing
cognitive burden in interpreting massive, unstructured data streams. The challenge lies in building a
unified, intelligent architecture that can dynamically route, analyze, and synthesize heterogeneous data
while maintaining interpretability and compliance transparency.
This paper addresses these issues by proposing a multi-agent diamond architecture, where each agent
performs specialized reasoning while maintaining centralized control and communication through the
King Agent and Risk Decision Agent.
4. Proposed Solution
The proposed framework introduces a diamond-shaped multi-agent architecture builtfor adaptive
financial intelligence. It begins with a King Agent, powered by FinBERT and Self-Distillation with No
Labels (SDNL), which ingests all incoming data and generates rich, domain-aware embeddings. The King Agent identifies the nature of each data point — transaction, loan, deposit, orKYC — and
delegates it to the corresponding sub-agent.
Each sub-agent uses a specialized model architecture suited for its task. The Transaction Agent
utilizesGraph Attention Networks (GAT) to uncovertransaction-level anomalies and entity
relationships. The Loan Evaluation Agent, based onGraph Neural Networks (GNN), identifies abnormal
borrowing orrepayment patterns. The KYC Verification Agent, leveraging a Decision Tree combined
with rule-based logic, verifies customeridentities and detects incomplete or inconsistent
documentation. The Deposit Agent, also based onGNN, detects cyclical deposit behavior indicative of
layering or smurfing.
The outcomes from all sub-agents merge into the Risk Decision Agent, a Deep Neural Combiner that
synthesizes results into a comprehensive risk score. Finally, the Virtual Assistant, built onGPT-4,
interacts with human analysts to explain findings, justify alerts, and answer contextual queries, thus
providing a transparent and interpretable AI-driven AML workflow.
5. Dataset and Preprocessing
The datasets used in this framework are sourced from Kaggle and include:
Anti-Money Laundering (AML) Transaction Dataset
Loan Default and Approval Dataset
Deposit Account ActivityDataset
KnowYour Customer (KYC) Dataset
Data preprocessing involves cleaning, transformation, and feature alignment across domains.
Numerical data such as transaction amounts and loan values are normalized using Min-Max scaling,
while categorical variables like account type or transaction purpose are encoded using embedding vectors. Missing values are handled through imputation and graph-based propagation to preserve
relational integrity.
For graph-based models (GNN, GAT), each entity (account, customer, or transaction) is represented as
a node, and edges represent relationships like fund transfers, shared addresses, or co-ownership. SDNL
embeddings are generated for each data sample to provide rich, unsupervised semantic
representations that feed into the King Agent.
