7. Methodology
The methodology involves five major stages: data ingestion, intelligent routing, domain-specific
reasoning, risk aggregation, and human interpretability.
Initially, all data enters through the King Agent, which employs FinBERT and SDNL to generate
contextual and unsupervised embeddings. Based on these embeddings, it classifies data types and
directs them to themostrelevant sub-agent.
Each Sub-Agent performs domain-specialized analysis using its respective model.
The Transaction Agent (GAT/GNN) identifies cross-network anomalies using attention-based edge
weights.
The Loan Evaluation Agent (GNN) models borrower–lenderrelationships and predicts high-risk lending
patterns.
The KYCAgent (Decision Tree + Rules) validates personal data, flags inconsistencies, and ensures
identity compliance.
The Deposit Agent (GNN) inspects cyclic deposit flows to detect structured layering patterns.
These outputs converge in the Risk Decision Agent, a Deep Neural Ensemble model thatfuses
probabilistic outputs from all sub-agents to produce an integrated risk score. This agent enhances
both precision and recall through multi-source learning fusion.
Finally,the GPT-4 Virtual Assistant interprets the model’s decision-making process, provides natural
language explanations, and assists compliance teams with real-time, query-based insights.
8. Results and Evaluation
The framework achieves a significantreduction in false positives compared to rule-based systems due
to its ensemble reasoning and graph contextualization. The use of FinBERT and SDNL enhances the
interpretability and adaptability of embeddings, while GAT and GNNmodels demonstrate high
accuracy in capturing relational patterns.
Preliminary evaluations on Kaggle datasets show:
Improved precision and recall (by~12%) overtraditional AML models.
Reduction of false positives by up to 25% due to multi-agent consensus.
Response interpretability improved throughGPT-4 explanations, reducing analyst reviewtime.
9. Business Impact
The diamond-shaped architecture provides a scalable and explainable AI-driven AML solution suitable
for real-world banking environments. It reduces operational costs by automating high-volume
monitoring, minimizes regulatory risks through transparent AI reasoning, and improves decision
accuracy via ensemble intelligence. The integration of a virtual assistant ensures that compliance
officers can interact directly with themodel, improving efficiency and trust in AI-assisted decisions.
10. Future Enhancements
Future work may involve extending the system with reinforcement learning-based self-optimization,
where agents autonomously update their thresholds and decision policies based on historical
performance. Additionally, federated learning can be incorporated to enable cross-institutional
collaboration without compromising data privacy. Integration with blockchain audit trails and
knowledge graphs could further enhance transparency and regulatory compliance.
11. Conclusion
This white paper introduces a comprehensive, intelligentframework that unifies multiple AI paradigms
into a single cohesive structure for AML detection. By combining FinBERT’s financial understanding,
SDNL’s self-supervised embeddings, graph-based reasoning, and conversational intelligence via GPT-4,
the proposed diamond-shaped architecture provides an adaptive, explainable, and future-ready solution
for financial crime detection. This model represents the next frontier in the evolution of AI-driven risk
management systems.
